    "id": "shoyo_41",
    "title": {
      "en": "Rakuho at His Deathbed"
    },
    "text": {
      "en": "Sometimes one is sincere and faithful, distressing oneself;\nOne�s suffering and pain is beyond words.\nSometimes misfortune arises, people being devoid of comprehension.\nAt the deathbed one condescends and retails cheaply:  at the very end one is most polite.\nTears welling out of painful guts, it is impossible to hide any more.\nNow, is there anyone who has cold eyes?\n\nWhen he was about to die, Rakuho addressed his assembly and said, �I have one matter to ask you about.  If you say, 'It is this,' you are putting another head on your own.  If you say, 'It isn't this,' you are looking for life by cutting off your head.�  The head monk said, �'The green mountain always lifts up its legs; you don't need to carry a lantern in the daylight.'�  Rakuho said, �What occasion is this to utter such a saying?�  A senior monk named Genj� stepped forward and said, �Apart from these two ways, I beg you, Master, not to ask.�  Rakuho said, �That's not enough.  Say some more.�  Genj� said, �I cannot say it fully.�  Rakuho said, �I don't care whether or not you can say it fully.�  Genj� said, �I feel just like �an attendant who has nothing to respond to his master�.�\nThat evening, Rakuho called Genj� to him and said, �Your response today had something quite reasonable.  You have to realize what our late master said, 'There are no dharmas before the eyes; [only] the consciousness is before the eyes.  It is not the Dharma before the eyes; It cannot be reached by eyes and ears.'  Which phrase is the guest?  Which phrase is the host?  If you can sort them out, I will transmit the bowl and robe to you.�  Genj� said, �I don't understand.�  Rakuho said, �You must understand.�  Genj� said, �I really don't understand.�  Rakuho shouted and said, �Miserable, miserable!�  \n[Another] monk asked, �What would you like to say, Master?�  Rakuho said, �The boat of compassion is not to be rowed over pure waves; it�s wasted labor releasing wooden geese down the precipitous strait.�\n\nWith the clouds as bait, with the moon as a hook, one fishes in the pure water;\nOld with age, solitary-hearted, one hasn�t caught a fish yet.\nAfter returning [to the common world, completing] the poem �Forsaking the Clamor�:\n[Lo,] the only sober one upon the Bekira River."
    },
