    "title": {
      "en": "Sepp� in Charge of Cooking�See Mumonkan Case 13"
    },
    "text": {
      "en": "Ice is chillier than water, blue comes out of indigo:\nOnly when one�s view excels that of one�s master, it is qualified to be transmitted;\nIf the child is brought up and cannot match the father, the family declines in one generation.  Tell me, who is it that snatches away the activities of one�s father?\n\nSepp� came to Tokusan and became in charge of cooking food.  One day, the lunch was late.  Tokusan came down to the hall carrying his bowls.  Sepp� said, �Old Master, the bell has not yet rung nor the drum sounded.  Where are you going with your bowls?�  Thereupon Tokusan went back to his room.  Sepp� told this to Gant�.  Gant� said, �Great Tokusan though he is, he has not yet realized the last word.�\n\tHearing of this, Tokusan sent his attendant to summon Gant� and then asked him, �Don't you approve of this old monk?�  Gant� whispered his intention.  Tokusan remained silent.\n\tSure enough, the next day, when Tokusan ascended the rostrum, his talk was quite different from usual.  Gant�, rubbing his hands together, laughed and said, �Wonderful!  How happy I am that our Old Man has realized the last word.  From now on he'll be subject to no one on earth.�\n\nThe last word � did you understand it or not?\nFather Tokusan and his son are terribly unclear.\nIn the assembly there is a guest from south of the River [Yangtze]: \nDon�t sing the song of partridges in front of him."
    },
